      needed: 'optional',
      note: 'Not required for entry. The Cranfield MSc provides motorsport context if your undergrad is from a non-motorsport university.',
      programs: [
        { name: 'MSc Advanced Motorsport Engineering', uni: 'Cranfield University', why: 'Vehicle Dynamics and tyre modelling modules in a motorsport context. Industry connections to Pirelli and F1 tyre engineering departments.', url: 'https://www.cranfield.ac.uk/som/courses/advanced-motorsport-engineering-msc' },
      ],
    },
    entry: { title: 'Junior Tyre Engineer', salary: '£28,000–£36,000 (UK, 2026)', teamTip: 'Also consider applying directly to Pirelli (the F1 tyre supplier) — their track engineers work closely with all 10 teams. Pirelli UK is based in Didcot, Oxfordshire, inside Motorsport Valley.', growth: 'Junior Tyre Engineer → Senior Tyre Engineer (4–6 yrs) → Tyre Performance Manager → Head of Tyre Strategy (12+ yrs).' },
  },
  {
    role: 'Race Engineer',
    hs: {
      courses: ['AP Physics C: Mechanics and E&M', 'AP Calculus BC', 'AP CS A'],
      activities: ['FIRST Robotics: take a team leadership or strategy role — Race Engineer is as much leadership as engineering', 'Study F1 races analytically: watch pit wall radio transcripts, study team strategy decisions', 'IMPORTANT: Race Engineer is not an entry-level job — plan to enter F1 as a Performance Engineer or Data Analyst first'],
      tip: 'Race Engineer is reached after 7-10 years in F1, not on day one. There are fewer than 20 Race Engineer seats across the entire sport. Focus your initial path on a specific technical discipline (vehicle dynamics, data, or aero) and work toward trackside roles over time.',
    },
    ug: {
      degree: 'Mechanical Engineering or Aerospace Engineering — any strong engineering background',
      why: 'Race Engineers come from all engineering backgrounds. Vehicle Dynamics and Performance Engineering routes are the most common paths to the pit wall. Pick a specific technical discipline for your undergraduate degree.',
      uk: ['Loughborough University — MEng Automotive/Mechanical Engineering', 'University of Southampton — MEng Mechanical or Aerospace', 'Oxford Brookes University — MEng Motorsport Engineering'],
      us: ['University of Michigan — Mechanical Engineering', 'Georgia Tech — Aerospace or Mechanical Engineering', 'Purdue — Mechanical Engineering', 'Rutgers — Mechanical & Aerospace (NJ in-state)'],
      focus: ['Mechanics, dynamics, and control theory', 'MATLAB/Simulink for vehicle performance simulation', 'Communication skills — Race Engineers speak to drivers and manage engineers simultaneously', 'Data analysis: telemetry processing, lap analysis'],
      fsae: 'Become the Race Engineer for the Formula SAE team at endurance events. Make real-time setup and strategy calls during the race. Communicate with the driver. This is the role compressed into a student competition.',
    },
    masters: {
      needed: 'not-needed',
      note: 'Not needed for the entry path. Focus on getting into F1 as a Performance or Data Engineer first — Race Engineer is where you end up, not where you start.',
      programs: [
        { name: 'MSc Motorsport Engineering', uni: 'Oxford Brookes University', why: 'The Lap Simulation & Race Engineering module is the most direct academic preparation for the Race Engineer role. Located inside Motorsport Valley near Williams, Red Bull, and Mercedes.', url: 'https://www.brookes.ac.uk/courses/postgraduate/motorsport-engineering' },
      ],
    },
    entry: { title: 'Junior Performance Engineer / Data Analyst → Race Engineer after 7-10 years', salary: '£28,000–£38,000 starting; £80,000–£120,000+ as Race Engineer', teamTip: 'Enter F1 via a Performance Engineer or Data Analyst role — all teams hire these. After 3-5 years, move toward trackside positions. The Race Engineer role is earned, not applied for on day one.', growth: 'Junior Performance Engineer → Senior Performance Engineer (4–6 yrs) → Trackside Performance Engineer → Race Engineer (7–10 yrs from graduation).' },
  },
];
---

<div class="f1g" id="f1-guide">

  <!-- ── Hero ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="f1g-hero">
    <div class="f1g-hero-text">
      <div class="f1g-hero-flag">🏁</div>
      <h2 class="f1g-hero-title">Working in Formula 1</h2>
      <p class="f1g-hero-sub">F1 employs 3,000+ engineers, data scientists, software developers, and strategists across 10 teams. This guide covers every job type, which universities produce F1 engineers, the Masters programs that open doors, and a step-by-step path from where you are now to a role on the grid.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="f1g-hero-stats">
      <div class="f1g-stat"><span class="f1g-stat-num">10</span><span class="f1g-stat-lbl">Teams</span></div>
      <div class="f1g-stat"><span class="f1g-stat-num">3,000+</span><span class="f1g-stat-lbl">Technical staff</span></div>
      <div class="f1g-stat"><span class="f1g-stat-num">24</span><span class="f1g-stat-lbl">Race weekends/yr</span></div>
      <div class="f1g-stat"><span class="f1g-stat-num">~23</span><span class="f1g-stat-lbl">Age at first F1 job</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Tab Nav ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <nav class="f1g-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="F1 career guide sections">
    <button class="f1g-tab f1g-tab--on" data-tab="jobs" role="tab" aria-selected="true">🏎 Jobs in F1</button>
    <button class="f1g-tab" data-tab="unis" role="tab" aria-selected="false">🎓 Universities</button>
    <button class="f1g-tab" data-tab="degrees" role="tab" aria-selected="false">📚 Degree Paths</button>
    <button class="f1g-tab" data-tab="masters" role="tab" aria-selected="false">🏫 Masters Programs</button>
    <button class="f1g-tab" data-tab="path" role="tab" aria-selected="false">🗺 Your Path</button>
    <button class="f1g-tab" data-tab="teams" role="tab" aria-selected="false">🔗 Team Portals</button>
  </nav>

  <!-- ── JOBS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="f1g-panel" id="f1g-jobs">
    <p class="f1g-intro">F1 teams are structured into 7-9 technical departments. Click any department to see the specific roles, what they do day to day, and what qualifications they need.</p>
    <div class="f1g-depts">
      {departments.map(dept => (
        <div class="f1g-dept">
          <button class="f1g-dept-hdr" data-dept={dept.id} aria-expanded="false">
            <span class="f1g-dept-icon">{dept.icon}</span>
            <div class="f1g-dept-info">
              <span class="f1g-dept-name">{dept.name}</span>
              <span class="f1g-dept-tag">{dept.tagline}</span>
            </div>
            <div class="f1g-dept-right">
              <span class="f1g-dept-count">{dept.roles.length} role{dept.roles.length !== 1 ? 's' : ''}</span>
              <span class="f1g-chevron">▼</span>
            </div>
          </button>
          <div class="f1g-dept-body" id={`f1g-dept-${dept.id}`} hidden>
            {dept.roles.map(role => (
              <div class="f1g-role">
                <div class="f1g-role-head">
                  <span class="f1g-role-title">{role.title}</span>
                  <div class="f1g-role-badges">
                    <span class={`f1g-badge f1g-badge--${role.demand}`}>{demandLabel[role.demand]}</span>
                    {role.mastersRequired && <span class="f1g-badge f1g-badge--masters">Masters Usually Required</span>}
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p class="f1g-role-desc">{role.description}</p>
                <div class="f1g-role-meta">
                  <div class="f1g-meta-item">
                    <span class="f1g-meta-lbl">Entry title</span>
                    <span class="f1g-meta-val">{role.entry}</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="f1g-meta-item">
                    <span class="f1g-meta-lbl">Degree</span>
                    <span class="f1g-meta-val">{role.degree}</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="f1g-role-tools">
                  <span class="f1g-meta-lbl">Key tools</span>
                  <div class="f1g-tags">
                    {role.tools.map(t => <span class="f1g-tag">{t}</span>)}
                  </div>
                </div>
                <button class="f1g-map-btn" data-role={role.title}>📍 Map My Path to {role.title} →</button>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="f1g-callout f1g-callout--tip">
      <strong>Formula SAE / Formula Student</strong> — Joining your university's Formula SAE team is arguably the single most impactful extracurricular activity for F1 recruitment. F1 engineers and recruiters specifically look for it on CVs. It signals hands-on systems thinking, teamwork under pressure, and genuine motorsport enthusiasm. Prioritise joining in your first semester of university.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── UNIVERSITIES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="f1g-panel f1g-panel--hide" id="f1g-unis">
    <p class="f1g-intro">Where you study matters enormously in F1. A 2024 analysis of ~97 F1 aerodynamics engineers on LinkedIn produced clear rankings — plus 7 of 10 F1 teams are within a 50-mile radius in central England.</p>

    <div class="f1g-callout f1g-callout--info">
      <strong>Motorsport Valley</strong> — 7 of the 10 current F1 teams are based in a 50-mile corridor in central England: Williams (Grove), Alpine (Enstone), Red Bull (Milton Keynes), Mercedes (Brackley), Mercedes HPP / engines (Brixworth), Aston Martin (Silverstone), and McLaren (Woking). Universities physically inside this corridor — Oxford Brookes, Cranfield, Loughborough, Coventry, Warwick — have structural placement advantages that US schools simply cannot match.
    </div>

    <h3 class="f1g-h3">Universities feeding F1 aerodynamics — ranked by % of engineers</h3>
    <p class="f1g-note">Data from LinkedIn analysis of ~97 F1 aerodynamics engineers across all teams (2024).</p>
    <div class="f1g-table-wrap">
      <table class="f1g-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>#</th><th>University</th><th>Country</th><th>Share of F1 Aero Engineers</th><th>Why</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="f1g-tr--top"><td>1</td><td><strong>University of Southampton</strong></td><td>🇬🇧 UK</td><td>~20%</td><td>Home of MSc Race Car Aerodynamics; largest university wind tunnel in UK</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>Imperial College London</td><td>🇬🇧 UK</td><td>~14%</td><td>Elite MEng Aeronautical Engineering; Mercedes research partnership</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>TU Delft</td><td>🇳🇱 NL</td><td>~10–11%</td><td>World-class CFD research; strong European motorsport pipeline</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>Loughborough University</td><td>🇬🇧 UK</td><td>~10%</td><td>MEng Automotive Engineering with Motorsport; dense F1 alumni network</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td>University of Cambridge</td><td>🇬🇧 UK</td><td>~9%</td><td>MEng Engineering (Aerospace / Aerothermal tracks); pure academic prestige</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6</td><td>University of Bristol</td><td>🇬🇧 UK</td><td>~6%</td><td>MEng Aerospace Engineering; strong fluid dynamics research</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7</td><td>Cranfield University</td><td>🇬🇧 UK</td><td>~5%</td><td>MSc only — specifically the Advanced Motorsport Engineering programme</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8</td><td>Politecnico di Milano</td><td>🇮🇹 IT</td><td>~2%</td><td>Main feeder for Ferrari and VCARB; Italian motorsport pipeline</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9</td><td>Politecnico di Torino</td><td>🇮🇹 IT</td><td>~2%</td><td>Italian automotive engineering; Ferrari adjacency</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10</td><td>University of Bath</td><td>🇬🇧 UK</td><td>~2%</td><td>MEng Mechanical Engineering; strong industry placement programme</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="f1g-h3">Team–University partnerships</h3>
    <div class="f1g-table-wrap">
      <table class="f1g-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>F1 Team</th><th>University Partner(s)</th><th>Nature</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Red Bull Racing</td><td>Oxford Brookes, Cranfield</td><td>Placement years, MSc industry projects</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mercedes-AMG F1</td><td>Imperial College London</td><td>Race strategy AI/ML research (RSRL model), placements</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Aston Martin F1</td><td>Cranfield, Coventry</td><td>Placement support, applied research</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ferrari</td><td>Politecnico di Milano, Bologna</td><td>Research collaborations, Maranello Academy</td></tr>
          <tr><td>McLaren</td><td>Loughborough, KAUST</td><td>Placement destinations; innovation partnership</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Williams</td><td>Oxford Brookes</td><td>Geographic proximity, placement pipeline</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Alpine</td><td>Loughborough</td><td>Placement partnership</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="f1g-h3">US universities with F1-relevant programs</h3>
    <p class="f1g-note">F1 teams do recruit from US universities — primarily for data, software, and strategy roles. The most common US path: strong undergrad → UK Masters → F1.</p>
    <div class="f1g-uni-us-grid">
      <div class="f1g-uni-us-card">
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-name">MIT</span>
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-dept">AeroAstro, Mechanical Engineering, CSAIL</span>
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-note">Best US aero/CS research base; exceptional CFD and AI fundamentals</span>
      </div>
      <div class="f1g-uni-us-card">
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-name">Georgia Tech</span>
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-dept">Aerospace Engineering (#2 US), ME, CS</span>
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-note">Strong Formula SAE culture; active F1 alumni community; cost-effective for out-of-state</span>
      </div>
      <div class="f1g-uni-us-card">
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-name">University of Michigan</span>
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-dept">Aerospace Engineering (#7 US), Mechanical</span>
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-note">MRacing Formula SAE team with F1 placement history; largest aero alumni network in US</span>
      </div>
      <div class="f1g-uni-us-card">
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-name">Purdue University</span>
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-dept">Aeronautics & Astronautics, Mechanical</span>
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-note">Very strong engineering fundamentals; NASA and defense pipeline that transfers to F1</span>
      </div>
      <div class="f1g-uni-us-card">
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-name">Stanford University</span>
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-dept">Mechanical + Aerospace, CS, Applied Math</span>
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-note">Best for data / software + engineering combination; strong for strategy roles</span>
      </div>
      <div class="f1g-uni-us-card f1g-uni-us-card--nj">
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-name">Rutgers University</span>
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-dept">Mechanical &amp; Aerospace Engineering (NJ in-state)</span>
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-note">Strong NJ in-state option: solid engineering programme, lower cost → strong GPA → UK Masters is a very viable route</span>
      </div>
      <div class="f1g-uni-us-card f1g-uni-us-card--nj">
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-name">Princeton University</span>
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-dept">Mechanical &amp; Aerospace Engineering</span>
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-note">Exceptional aero/fluid dynamics research if admitted; strong local NJ option for the F1 academic-research path</span>
      </div>
      <div class="f1g-uni-us-card f1g-uni-us-card--nj">
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-name">Cornell University</span>
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-dept">Mechanical Engineering, Aerospace</span>
        <span class="f1g-uni-us-note">Strong Ivy engineering; active Formula SAE team; accessible from NJ</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="f1g-note" style="margin-top:0.5rem">Cards with a green left border are NJ-accessible options.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── DEGREES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="f1g-panel f1g-panel--hide" id="f1g-degrees">
    <p class="f1g-intro">Each engineering degree maps to specific F1 roles. The table below shows which degree opens which doors, whether a Masters is typically needed, and the strongest universities for each stream.</p>
    <div class="f1g-table-wrap">
      <table class="f1g-table f1g-table--degrees">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Degree</th><th>Primary F1 Roles</th><th>Masters Needed?</th><th>Top UK Universities</th><th>Top US Universities</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Aerospace Engineering</strong></td>
            <td>Aerodynamicist, CFD Engineer, Structures Engineer, Simulation Engineer</td>
            <td><span class="f1g-badge f1g-badge--masters">Usually Yes</span></td>
            <td>Southampton, Imperial, Cambridge, Bristol, Bath</td>
            <td>MIT, Georgia Tech, Purdue, Michigan</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Mechanical Engineering</strong></td>
            <td>Design Engineer, Vehicle Dynamics, Powertrain, Composites, Tyre Engineer</td>
            <td><span class="f1g-badge f1g-badge--maybe">Recommended</span></td>
            <td>Loughborough, Southampton, Imperial, Cambridge, Bath</td>
            <td>MIT, Michigan, Georgia Tech, Purdue, Cornell, Rutgers</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Automotive / Motorsport Engineering</strong></td>
            <td>Vehicle Dynamics, Race Engineer, Design Engineer</td>
            <td><span class="f1g-badge f1g-badge--maybe">Recommended</span></td>
            <td>Loughborough, Oxford Brookes, Coventry</td>
            <td>Limited US options — consider UK for undergrad</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Electrical / Electronic Engineering</strong></td>
            <td>Electronics Engineer, ERS / Controls Engineer, Powertrain (hybrid)</td>
            <td><span class="f1g-badge f1g-badge--no">Often Not Required</span></td>
            <td>Imperial, Southampton, Loughborough, Cranfield (MSc)</td>
            <td>MIT, Georgia Tech, Michigan, Purdue</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Mechatronics / Systems Engineering</strong></td>
            <td>Controls Engineer, ERS Engineer, Simulation Engineer</td>
            <td><span class="f1g-badge f1g-badge--no">Often Not Required</span></td>
            <td>Loughborough, Bath, Warwick</td>
            <td>MIT, Carnegie Mellon, Georgia Tech</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Computer Science</strong></td>
            <td>Software Developer, Data Engineer, Strategy Engineer, Simulation Software</td>
            <td><span class="f1g-badge f1g-badge--no">Rarely Required</span></td>
            <td>Imperial, Cambridge, UCL, Southampton</td>
            <td>MIT, Stanford, Cornell, Rutgers, Princeton</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Mathematics / Statistics</strong></td>
            <td>Strategy Engineer, Data Scientist, Simulation Engineer, Performance Analyst</td>
            <td><span class="f1g-badge f1g-badge--no">Rarely Required</span></td>
            <td>Cambridge, Imperial, Oxford, Warwick</td>
            <td>MIT, Princeton, Stanford, Columbia</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Materials Science</strong></td>
            <td>Composites Engineer, Materials Engineer</td>
            <td><span class="f1g-badge f1g-badge--maybe">For Senior Roles</span></td>
            <td>Imperial, Oxford, Bath, Manchester</td>
            <td>MIT, Northwestern, Michigan</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Physics</strong></td>
            <td>CFD Engineer, Simulation Engineer, Strategy / Data</td>
            <td><span class="f1g-badge f1g-badge--masters">Yes — convert to Eng MSc</span></td>
            <td>Cambridge, Imperial, Oxford, Durham</td>
            <td>MIT, Caltech, Princeton</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="f1g-callout f1g-callout--tip">
      <strong>Which degree is most versatile for F1?</strong> Mechanical Engineering keeps the most doors open across departments. Aerospace Engineering is the highest-density path into aero/CFD. Computer Science is growing fastest as F1 becomes more data-driven. If you are strong in maths and physics and enjoy both coding and engineering, a Mechanical or Aerospace degree with strong programming coursework (Python, MATLAB, C++) covers the most ground.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── MASTERS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="f1g-panel f1g-panel--hide" id="f1g-masters">
    <p class="f1g-intro">A postgraduate degree is effectively required for most F1 engineering roles. These five programs are the most widely recognised by F1 teams — ranked by F1 hiring relevance.</p>
    {masterPrograms.map(mp => (
      <div class="f1g-mc">
        <div class="f1g-mc-head">
          <div class="f1g-mc-title-block">
            <h3 class="f1g-mc-name">{mp.name}</h3>
            <span class="f1g-mc-uni">{mp.uni}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="f1g-mc-score">
            <span class="f1g-mc-score-lbl">F1 Relevance</span>
            <span class="f1g-mc-dots">{'●'.repeat(mp.f1Score)}{'○'.repeat(5 - mp.f1Score)}</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="f1g-mc-meta">
          <span class="f1g-mc-meta-item">📍 {mp.location}</span>
          <span class="f1g-mc-meta-item">⏱ {mp.duration}</span>
          <span class="f1g-mc-meta-item">💷 {mp.cost}</span>
        </div>
        <p class="f1g-mc-focus">{mp.focus}</p>
        <div class="f1g-mc-modules">
          <span class="f1g-meta-lbl">Key modules</span>
          <div class="f1g-tags">
            {mp.modules.map(m => <span class="f1g-tag">{m}</span>)}
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="f1g-callout f1g-callout--standout">
          <strong>Standout fact:</strong> {mp.standout}
        </div>
        <a class="f1g-mc-link" href={mp.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View program →</a>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <!-- ── PATH ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="f1g-panel f1g-panel--hide" id="f1g-path">
    <p class="f1g-intro">Select a specific role to see your step-by-step path from high school through university, Masters, and into F1. Or use the general tracks below.</p>

    <!-- Role selector -->
    <div class="f1g-rp-bar">
      <label class="f1g-rp-label" for="f1g-rp-select">Choose a role for a personalised roadmap:</label>
      <div class="f1g-rp-controls">
        <select class="f1g-rp-select" id="f1g-rp-select">
          <option value="">— Select a role —</option>
          <optgroup label="Aerodynamics">
            <option>Aerodynamicist</option>
            <option>CFD Engineer</option>
            <option>Wind Tunnel Test Engineer</option>
          </optgroup>
          <optgroup label="Vehicle Dynamics">
            <option>Vehicle Dynamics Engineer</option>
            <option>Driver-in-Loop Simulation Engineer</option>
          </optgroup>
          <optgroup label="Powertrain & Engine">
            <option>Powertrain Engineer</option>
            <option>Engine Calibration Engineer</option>
          </optgroup>
          <optgroup label="Electronics & Controls">
            <option>Electronics Engineer</option>
            <option>Control Systems / ERS Engineer</option>
          </optgroup>
          <optgroup label="Data & Software">
            <option>Performance Data Engineer</option>
            <option>Strategy Engineer</option>
            <option>Simulation Software Engineer</option>
            <option>General Software Developer</option>
          </optgroup>
          <optgroup label="Structures & Race Ops">
            <option>Composites Design Engineer</option>
            <option>Tyre Engineer</option>
            <option>Race Engineer</option>
          </optgroup>
        </select>
        <button class="f1g-rp-clear" id="f1g-rp-clear" hidden>✕ Clear</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Role-specific paths (rendered at build time, shown/hidden by JS) -->
    <div id="f1g-rp-display">
      {rolePaths.map(rp => (
        <div class="f1g-rp-path" data-role={rp.role} hidden>
          <div class="f1g-rp-heading">
            <h3 class="f1g-rp-title">Path to: <strong>{rp.role}</strong></h3>
          </div>
          <div class="f1g-timeline">

            <div class="f1g-phase f1g-phase--now">
              <div class="f1g-phase-dot"></div>
              <div class="f1g-phase-content">
                <div class="f1g-phase-label">PHASE 1 — NOW (High School)</div>
                <h4 class="f1g-phase-title">Courses to take</h4>
                <ul class="f1g-phase-list">
                  {rp.hs.courses.map(c => <li>{c}</li>)}
                </ul>
                <h4 class="f1g-phase-title" style="margin-top:0.75rem">Activities</h4>
                <ul class="f1g-phase-list">
                  {rp.hs.activities.map(a => <li>{a}</li>)}
                </ul>
                <div class="f1g-rp-tip">💡 {rp.hs.tip}</div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="f1g-phase">
              <div class="f1g-phase-dot"></div>
              <div class="f1g-phase-content">
                <div class="f1g-phase-label">PHASE 2 — UNDERGRADUATE (4 years)</div>
                <h4 class="f1g-phase-title">{rp.ug.degree}</h4>
                <p class="f1g-phase-sub">{rp.ug.why}</p>
                <div class="f1g-rp-uni-row">
                  <div class="f1g-rp-uni-col">
                    <span class="f1g-meta-lbl">🇬🇧 UK Universities</span>
                    <ul class="f1g-rp-uni-list">
                      {rp.ug.uk.map(u => <li>{u}</li>)}
                    </ul>
                  </div>
                  <div class="f1g-rp-uni-col">
                    <span class="f1g-meta-lbl">🇺🇸 US Universities</span>
                    <ul class="f1g-rp-uni-list">
                      {rp.ug.us.map(u => <li>{u}</li>)}
                    </ul>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <span class="f1g-meta-lbl" style="margin-top:0.75rem;display:block">Focus areas within the degree</span>
                <ul class="f1g-phase-list">
                  {rp.ug.focus.map(f => <li>{f}</li>)}
                </ul>
                <div class="f1g-rp-fsae">🏎 <strong>Formula SAE:</strong> {rp.ug.fsae}</div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class={`f1g-phase ${rp.masters.needed === 'not-needed' ? 'f1g-phase--dim' : ''}`}>
              <div class="f1g-phase-dot"></div>
              <div class="f1g-phase-content">
                <div class="f1g-phase-label">PHASE 3 — MASTERS / FURTHER STUDY</div>
                <h4 class="f1g-phase-title">
                  {rp.masters.needed === 'required' && <span class="f1g-rp-needed f1g-rp-needed--req">Required</span>}
                  {rp.masters.needed === 'recommended' && <span class="f1g-rp-needed f1g-rp-needed--rec">Recommended</span>}
                  {rp.masters.needed === 'optional' && <span class="f1g-rp-needed f1g-rp-needed--opt">Optional</span>}
                  {rp.masters.needed === 'not-needed' && <span class="f1g-rp-needed f1g-rp-needed--no">Not Required</span>}
                </h4>
                <p class="f1g-phase-sub">{rp.masters.note}</p>
                {rp.masters.programs.length > 0 && (
                  <div class="f1g-rp-programs">
                    {rp.masters.programs.map(p => (
                      <div class="f1g-rp-program">
                        <div class="f1g-rp-prog-head">
                          <span class="f1g-rp-prog-name">{p.name}</span>
                          <span class="f1g-rp-prog-uni">{p.uni}</span>
                        </div>
                        <p class="f1g-rp-prog-why">{p.why}</p>
                        <a class="f1g-rp-prog-link" href={p.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View program →</a>
                      </div>
                    ))}
                  </div>
                )}
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="f1g-phase f1g-phase--f1">
              <div class="f1g-phase-dot"></div>
              <div class="f1g-phase-content">
                <div class="f1g-phase-label">PHASE 4 — F1 ENTRY</div>
                <h4 class="f1g-phase-title">{rp.entry.title}</h4>
                <div class="f1g-rp-entry-grid">
                  <div class="f1g-meta-item">
                    <span class="f1g-meta-lbl">Starting salary</span>
                    <span class="f1g-meta-val">{rp.entry.salary}</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="f1g-meta-item">
                    <span class="f1g-meta-lbl">10-year path</span>
                    <span class="f1g-meta-val">{rp.entry.growth}</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="f1g-rp-tip f1g-rp-tip--team">🏁 {rp.entry.teamTip}</div>
              </div>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="f1g-or-divider" id="f1g-or-divider">
      <span>or browse by general track</span>
    </div>

    <div id="f1g-generic-tracks">
    <div class="f1g-path-tracks">
      <button class="f1g-track-btn f1g-track-btn--on" data-track="eng">⚙️ Engineering Track (Aero / VD / Powertrain)</button>
      <button class="f1g-track-btn" data-track="tech">💻 Tech Track (Software / Data / Strategy)</button>
    </div>

    <!-- Engineering Track -->
    <div class="f1g-track" id="f1g-track-eng">
      <div class="f1g-timeline">

        <div class="f1g-phase f1g-phase--now">
          <div class="f1g-phase-dot"></div>
          <div class="f1g-phase-content">
            <div class="f1g-phase-label">NOW — 11th Grade (2026-2027)</div>
            <h4 class="f1g-phase-title">Build the foundation</h4>
            <ul class="f1g-phase-list">
              <li>Take <strong>AP Physics C: Mechanics</strong> and <strong>AP Physics C: Electricity &amp; Magnetism</strong> — these are the direct prerequisites for university fluid dynamics and electromagnetism</li>
              <li>Take <strong>AP Calculus BC</strong> — university aero and dynamics courses assume multivariable calculus from day one</li>
              <li>Robotics (FIRST, which you already do) is directly relevant: controls, mechanical design, systems thinking — name it prominently on university applications</li>
              <li>Research <strong>Formula SAE</strong> teams at your target universities — know which schools have active teams before applying</li>
              <li>Watch: MIT OpenCourseWare 16.100 Aerodynamics — free intro to the actual subject area</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="f1g-phase">
          <div class="f1g-phase-dot"></div>
          <div class="f1g-phase-content">
            <div class="f1g-phase-label">UNDERGRAD — 2027 to 2031 (~4 years)</div>
            <h4 class="f1g-phase-title">Aerospace or Mechanical Engineering degree</h4>
            <p class="f1g-phase-sub">Target universities: Georgia Tech, Michigan, Purdue, MIT, or Cornell. For NJ cost-effectiveness: Rutgers or Princeton.</p>
            <ul class="f1g-phase-list">
              <li><strong>Join Formula SAE in semester 1</strong> — this is the most important extracurricular action you can take. Work in aero, vehicle dynamics, or electronics sub-teams. F1 recruiters treat this as near-equivalent to an internship.</li>
              <li>Focus coursework on: fluid dynamics, heat transfer, structural mechanics, controls systems, MATLAB / Python</li>
              <li>Pursue an aerospace or automotive internship in years 2-3 (aerospace OEMs, NASA, DoD labs all build directly applicable skills)</li>
              <li>Aim for strong GPA in core engineering modules — especially fluid mechanics and computational methods</li>
              <li>If at a UK university (Southampton, Loughborough): the 4-year MEng with industrial placement year gives an integrated Masters + trackside experience in one degree</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="f1g-phase">
          <div class="f1g-phase-dot"></div>
          <div class="f1g-phase-content">
            <div class="f1g-phase-label">MASTERS — 2031 to 2032 (1-2 years)</div>
            <h4 class="f1g-phase-title">Motorsport or aerospace Masters in the UK</h4>
            <p class="f1g-phase-sub">This is the step that moves you from "strong engineer" to "F1 hire." Most UK Masters include industry projects placed directly inside F1 teams.</p>
            <ul class="f1g-phase-list">
              <li><strong>Aero / CFD path</strong>: Southampton MSc Race Car Aerodynamics — the most direct route to an F1 aero role in the world</li>
              <li><strong>Broad engineering path</strong>: Cranfield MSc Advanced Motorsport Engineering — covers aero + VD + powertrain + electronics in one year; best if you want flexibility across departments</li>
              <li><strong>Vehicle dynamics path</strong>: Oxford Brookes MSc Motorsport Engineering — physically inside Motorsport Valley, Red Bull placement partner</li>
              <li><strong>Budget-conscious path</strong>: TU Delft MSc Aerospace (Netherlands) — comparable F1 hiring rates, lower cost than UK, strong CFD research</li>
              <li>Apply to F1 early careers / graduate positions in <strong>August-October</strong> — this is the critical window for the following year's intake</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="f1g-phase f1g-phase--f1">
          <div class="f1g-phase-dot"></div>
          <div class="f1g-phase-content">
            <div class="f1g-phase-label">F1 ENTRY — 2032-2033 (Age ~23-24)</div>
            <h4 class="f1g-phase-title">First role on the grid</h4>
            <ul class="f1g-phase-list">
              <li>Entry title: Junior Aerodynamicist / Junior CFD Analyst / Junior Vehicle Dynamics Engineer / Junior Performance Engineer</li>
              <li><strong>UK work visa</strong>: After graduating from a top-50 globally ranked US university (MIT, Georgia Tech, Michigan all qualify), you are eligible for the <strong>UK High Potential Individual (HPI) Visa</strong> — 2-3 years of unsponsored work authorization. This is a major advantage: you do not need the F1 team to sponsor you from day one.</li>
              <li>Starting salaries: £30,000-£45,000 for UK-based junior roles (2026 market)</li>
              <li>US-based entry: Consider Haas F1 (Kannapolis, NC) or Cadillac F1 (Indianapolis + Silverstone) for roles that do not require immediate relocation to the UK</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Tech Track -->
    <div class="f1g-track f1g-track--hide" id="f1g-track-tech">
      <div class="f1g-timeline">

        <div class="f1g-phase f1g-phase--now">
          <div class="f1g-phase-dot"></div>
          <div class="f1g-phase-content">
            <div class="f1g-phase-label">NOW — 11th Grade (2026-2027)</div>
            <h4 class="f1g-phase-title">Build the data and programming foundation</h4>
            <ul class="f1g-phase-list">
              <li>Take <strong>AP CS A</strong> (Java) and follow up with Python self-study — Python is the dominant language across F1 data engineering and strategy</li>
              <li>Take <strong>AP Calculus BC</strong> and <strong>AP Statistics</strong> — strategy and data science roles require probability, optimisation, and statistical modelling</li>
              <li>Explore the public <strong>FastF1 Python library</strong> — it pulls official F1 telemetry and session data. Building your own tyre degradation model or pit stop optimiser is exactly the kind of portfolio work F1 data teams respond to</li>
              <li>Robotics coding and controls experience (which you already have) is highly relevant to simulation software roles</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="f1g-phase">
          <div class="f1g-phase-dot"></div>
          <div class="f1g-phase-content">
            <div class="f1g-phase-label">UNDERGRAD — 2027 to 2031 (~4 years)</div>
            <h4 class="f1g-phase-title">Computer Science, Mathematics, or CS + Engineering</h4>
            <p class="f1g-phase-sub">Target universities: MIT, Stanford, Cornell, Georgia Tech, Rutgers (NJ in-state). A CS + Mechanical double major is the ideal combination.</p>
            <ul class="f1g-phase-list">
              <li>Join Formula SAE in semester 1 — work in the data, electronics, or controls sub-team. Same value as the engineering track.</li>
              <li>Build a motorsport data portfolio using open F1 data (FastF1): tyre models, race strategy simulations, telemetry visualisations. Put this on GitHub.</li>
              <li>Coursework priorities: algorithms and data structures, databases, machine learning, distributed systems, numerical methods, optimisation</li>
              <li>Software internships in years 2-4: sports analytics firms, aerospace / defense tech, automotive OEMs (all build directly applicable skills)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="f1g-phase">
          <div class="f1g-phase-dot"></div>
          <div class="f1g-phase-content">
            <div class="f1g-phase-label">ENTRY OR MASTERS — 2031 to 2032</div>
            <h4 class="f1g-phase-title">Many data / software roles do not require a Masters</h4>
            <ul class="f1g-phase-list">
              <li>A strong CS or Maths undergrad + portfolio + Formula SAE data experience can be sufficient to apply directly to F1 data engineering, software, or strategy roles</li>
              <li>If you want to strengthen the application: 1-year MSc at <strong>Imperial College (MSc Computing — ML track)</strong> or <strong>University of Southampton (MSc Data Science)</strong> adds significant signal</li>
              <li>Apply to F1 in August-October for the following year's intake</li>
              <li>Relevant starting roles: Junior Data Engineer, Performance Analyst, Junior Strategy Engineer, Graduate Software Developer</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="f1g-phase f1g-phase--f1">
          <div class="f1g-phase-dot"></div>
          <div class="f1g-phase-content">
            <div class="f1g-phase-label">F1 ENTRY — 2031-2032 (Age ~22-23)</div>
            <h4 class="f1g-phase-title">First role on the grid — faster than the engineering track</h4>
            <ul class="f1g-phase-list">
              <li>The tech track can reach F1 entry 1-2 years earlier than the engineering path because a Masters is often not required</li>
              <li>Most accessible US entry points: Haas F1 (US base), Cadillac F1 (GM / Andretti — US brand, actively hiring), or remote data / software roles that are growing across most teams</li>
              <li>The UK HPI Visa still applies if you graduated from a top-50 globally ranked US university</li>
              <li>Growth path: Data Engineer → Senior Performance Data Engineer → Strategy Engineer → Head of Strategy (10-15 year trajectory)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

    </div> <!-- /f1g-generic-tracks -->

    <div class="f1g-callout f1g-callout--tip" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <strong>Application timing is everything.</strong> F1 teams open graduate and placement positions in <strong>August-October</strong> for the following year's intake. Set a calendar reminder. Check all 10 team career portals in this window every year from your second year of university. Many positions are filled before December.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── TEAMS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="f1g-panel f1g-panel--hide" id="f1g-teams">
    <p class="f1g-intro">All 10 current F1 teams hire for technical, data, and commercial roles. Check these portals every August-October for graduate and placement openings. Teams marked with a 🇺🇸 are most accessible for US-based applicants.</p>
    <div class="f1g-teams-grid">
      {teams.map(team => (
        <div class="f1g-team-card" style={`border-left-color: ${team.color}`}>
          <div class="f1g-team-head">
            <span class="f1g-team-name">
              {team.usAccessible && <span class="f1g-us-badge">🇺🇸</span>}
              {team.name}
            </span>
            <span class="f1g-team-base">📍 {team.base}</span>
          </div>
          <p class="f1g-team-focus">{team.focus}</p>
          {team.note && <p class="f1g-team-note">{team.note}</p>}
          <a class="f1g-team-link" href={team.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open careers page →</a>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="f1g-callout f1g-callout--info" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
      <strong>Also look at:</strong> <a href="https://formulacareers.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">formulacareers.com</a> and <a href="https://tracksidecareers.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">tracksidecareers.com</a> — these aggregate job listings from all F1 teams, Mercedes HPP, Red Bull Powertrains, and F1 suppliers (Pirelli, Brembo, etc.) in one place. Bookmark both.
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

<style>
  /* ── Container ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
